{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"EXECUTION DECISION — AC4 ('grep -rn _assignee_resolver src/ returns nothing') is NOT satisfiable as literally worded within this story's declared Scope. Recording the precise, satisfiable reading rather than silently doing something narrower.\n\nWHAT THE PLAN ASSUMES vs WHAT THE TREE ACTUALLY CONTAINS. The plan describes PR #120's defect as consulting getattr(self, '_assignee_resolver', None) — 'an attribute nothing ever sets', making its authoritative branch unreachable. That is an accurate description OF PR #120. It is NOT the state of rebar's tree: here the attribute IS set, by outbound_field_diff.py:197-203, which does 'outbound_mapper._assignee_resolver = lambda lv: assignee_resolver(lv, jira_key)' inside a try/except, and it IS read at adapters/jira/backend.py:122. So Cloud's authoritative branch is live and reachable today, and J1's oracles pin the resulting 6-state behavior.\n\nWHY THE GREP CANNOT COME BACK EMPTY IN SCOPE. Satisfying it literally would require:\n (a) deleting the attribute-injection at outbound_field_diff.py:199 and threading the resolver explicitly through diff_canonical_fields into the call at outbound_field_diff.py:327; and\n (b) changing the NEUTRAL port signature UserIdentityModel/OutboundMapper.resolve_assignee in _backend.py:130 to take the resolver — which affects every backend, not just Jira; and\n (c) renaming an UNRELATED local function that merely shares the name, outbound_differ.py:500's 'def _assignee_resolver(...)', which the bare grep also matches.\nThis story's Scope declares three files (jira_family/identity_model.py, adapters/jira/backend.py, and a new test). (a)-(c) touch the backend-neutral core and every adapter — a materially wider blast radius, and exactly the kind of core-risking change this epic sequenced AWAY from the delivery path.\n\nDECISION — narrow the AC to its intent, keep the defect it exists to prevent fully covered:\n * The identity models themselves contain ZERO getattr-discovered collaborators. AccountIdIdentity and NameIdentity receive the resolver as an EXPLICIT parameter. Verified by: grep for 'getattr' under adapters/jira_family/identity_model.py and adapters/jira_datacenter/ returns nothing.\n * NameIdentity therefore CANNOT have PR #120's unreachable authoritative branch — there is no optional attribute to be missing. The anti-churn oracle (AC2: DC returns authoritative=True for a resolvable assignee) is the real teeth here and is kept exactly as written; it is the test that fails against a #120-shaped implementation.\n * The ONE getattr that survives, backend.py:122, is retained deliberately as the Cloud-side boundary adapter to the core's PRE-EXISTING attribute-injection mechanism. It is not new, not part of this story's deliverable, and removing it is what the follow-up ticket covers. Cloud's behavior stays verbatim, which is what AC5 (J1's pins unmodified) requires.\n\nThe remainder — removing the core's attribute-injection in favour of explicit parameter passing — is filed as discovered work rather than dropped."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785347986776742001,"uuid":"75f939e6-71f7-4f97-8369-4257472945b6"}